{"id":54230,"title":"[PDF] Direct Volume Rendering | CGL @ ETHZ","url":"https://cgl.ethz.ch/teaching/former/scivis_07/Notes/stuff/StuttgartCourse/VIS-Modules-06-Direct_Volume_Rendering.pdf","final_url":"https://cgl.ethz.ch/teaching/former/scivis_07/Notes/stuff/StuttgartCourse/VIS-Modules-06-Direct_Volume_Rendering.pdf","year":null,"kind":"link","source":"link","doi":null,"arxiv":null,"sha256":"0da527b8d4745a44357ffa499de2fc232436d275e81392112c13046b16b32723","ctype":"application/pdf","text_src":"url","fetched_at":1793070034.9769871,"text_chars":50703,"abstract":"cgl.ethz.ch\n\nhttps://cgl.ethz.ch › teaching › former › scivis_07 › Notes › stuff › StuttgartCourse › VIS-Modules-06-Direct_Volume_Rendering.pdf\n\nPDF Direct Volume Rendering - ETH Z\nDirect Volume Rendering The idea of Direct Volume Rendering (DVR) is to get a 3D representation of the volume data directly. The data is considered to represent a semi-transparent light-emitting medium. Therefore also gaseous phenomena can be simulated. The approaches in DVR are based on the laws of physics (emission, absorption, scattering).","file":null,"marks":["unofficial"],"links":["https://cgl.ethz.ch/teaching/former/scivis_07/Notes/stuff/StuttgartCourse/VIS-Modules-06-Direct_Volume_Rendering.pdf"],"cited_by":[{"path":"902-adaptive-octree-construction-with-2-1-balance-enforcement.md","name":"Adaptive Octree Construction with 2:1 Balance Enforcement","k":26},{"path":"1180-interactive-volume-visualization-via-multi-resolution-hash-e.md","name":"Interactive Volume Visualization Via Multi-Resolution Hash Encoding Based Neural Representation","k":7},{"path":"1194-dual-object-background-blending.md","name":"Dual-Object Background Blending","k":6},{"path":"877-projected-tetrahedra.md","name":"Projected Tetrahedra","k":4},{"path":"958-volumetric-isosurface-rendering-with-intersection-refinement.md","name":"Volumetric Isosurface Rendering with Intersection Refinement","k":6},{"path":"927-emission-absorption-model.md","name":"Emi